  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently sliding across the Northern Plains into \nthe Upper Mississippi Valley with a shortwave trough moving towards \nthe Lower Mississippi Valley. At the surface, a low is over the \nGreat Lakes with a cold front extending down through Illinois into \nthe Lower Mississippi Valley. In our area, breezy northerly winds \ncontinue with gusts up to 25-30 mph. Cooler conditions have settled \nin with highs topping out in the 40s across much of our area \nwith portions of southeast KS seeing readings in the lower 50s.\nAs we move into tonight and Monday morning, low temperatures \nwill drop into the lower 20s in central KS with portions of \nsouthern KS seeing readings in the upper 20s. \n\nBy Monday morning, flow aloft will shift to zonal over the Central \nPlains. Expansive surface high pressure will settle into the \nNorthern Plains with surface ridging extending down to the Southern \nPlains. This will result in light winds over our area with similar \ntemperatures to today on Monday afternoon as highs are forecast in \nthe 40s. \n\nCloud cover will start to increase Monday night as another trough \nwill slide across the Plains on Tuesday. Minimal impacts are \nexpected with this system, though if any precipitation does develop, \nit will stay in far western Kansas, as the column remains dry over \neastern Kansas. This trough will bring another cold snap to the \nregion as below normal temperatures for this time of year are \nprogged for Tuesday and Tuesday night with highs in the lower 40s \nand lows in the lower 20s. \n\nThis cold snap will be short-lived as temperatures start to rebound \nafter Wednesday as upper ridging builds in with thickness responding \naccordingly and increasing. By Friday, highs will return to the 50s \nwith upper 50s forecast for next weekend. Similarly, low \ntemperatures are expected to rise back into the 30s. Mostly dry \nconditions are likely through this period as well.",
